course: dictionary
lesson: 9489
title: Steamy and the clues around it
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Steamy is an adjective, a describing word. It means full of steam, the hot water vapour you see rising from a bath, or hot and wet, like the air in a rainforest. When you meet it, look for clues in the words around it.
explain: «After a steamy bath or shower, turn on the fan and open the door; damp air makes mould grow.» The fan, the open door and the damp air all point to hot, wet air full of steam. So steamy means full of that hot, damp air.
explain: Try your meaning in the sentence. «Titanoboa lived in a warm, steamy world of rivers» makes sense with "warm and wet", so you have found the right meaning. Words like steamy can also be used in phrases such as "hot and steamy".
fact: Steamy is an adjective: it describes a noun, as in "a steamy bathroom" or "a steamy jungle".
fact: Steam is hot water vapour, so steamy means full of it.
fact: A hot and steamy place, like a rainforest, is warm and wet.
fact: When steam meets something cold, such as a mirror, it turns back into water drops, which is why a bathroom mirror gets misty.
fact: Good phrases to remember are steamy bathroom, steamy windows and hot and steamy.
exercise: «After a steamy bath or shower, turn on the fan and open the door.» What does steamy mean here? | Loud and noisy | Empty and dry | Cold and icy | *Full of hot water vapour
exercise: «Titanoboa lived in a warm, steamy world of rivers.» What does steamy suggest about this world? | Dusty and bare | *Warm and wet | Cold and dry | Dark and quiet
exercise: «You write "level" on a steamy window.» What does steamy mean about the window? | Frozen solid | *Covered in mist from warm, wet air | Painted in bright colours | Cracked and broken
test: «Blowing on soup sweeps away the warm, steamy air above it.» What is the steamy air? | *Hot air full of steam | Cold air from the fridge | Air with no water in it at all | Dry air from a fan
test: In the phrase "a steamy jungle", what type of word is steamy? | Verb | Adverb | *Adjective | Noun
exam: Lily says steamy means cold and frosty. Who is right, and why? | Neither is right, because steamy means noisy | *Lily is wrong, because steamy means full of hot, wet steam | Lily is right, because steamy means frosty | Both are right, because steam is always cold
